---
// Two doors — section 5 of the home-page skeleton (mt#3827).
// Locked copy, principal-approved 2026-08-07. Presented as equals:
// same heading level, same visual weight, no default/alternate framing.
---

<section id="doors" class="doors-section" aria-labelledby="doors-heading">
  <div class="doors-inner">
    <header class="doors-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two doors</p>
      <h2 id="doors-heading" class="doors-headline">
        Run your organization. Extend yourself.
      </h2>
      <p class="doors-sub">
        Minsky is useful in two different shapes. Neither is the main one — pick whichever
        matches what you are trying to do.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="doors-grid">
      <article class="door">
        <h3 class="door-title">Run your organization</h3>
        <p class="door-body">
          Agents handle a well-scoped task impressively and a sustained project badly. That's
          not a model problem — it's a missing-organization problem. Software work holds
          together because of structure around the coding: work is tracked, changes are
          isolated, review is independent, lessons are recorded, and someone is responsible for
          decisions. Run agents without that structure and the human becomes all of it at once —
          dispatcher, reviewer, janitor of half-finished branches — which doesn't scale past one.
          Minsky builds the structure into the environment. Every task gets an isolated workspace
          with its own branch and scope. Every change is reviewed by a separate agent with a
          different model and no stake in the code it's judging, and merges wait until review
          converges. Standards are enforced by hooks that stop a bad commit the same way for an
          agent as for a human — no rules an agent can forget under pressure. What the system
          learns is kept and surfaced instead of relearned. And decisions that are genuinely
          yours arrive as structured questions with the context to answer them. The result: you
          state what should happen; the environment carries it to reviewed, merged work.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="door">
        <h3 class="door-title">Extend yourself</h3>
        <p class="door-body">
          If you run serious work through agents, you know the shape of the problem: eight
          sessions across five projects, chats everywhere, and the only place the whole picture
          exists is your head. Note tools store what you know, not what's happening; project
          trackers model tasks, not attention; each agent session is blind to the others. So
          every morning starts with reconstruction — what was I doing, what's stuck, where do I
          start? Minsky keeps that picture outside your skull. The cockpit shows the live state
          of everything in flight and surfaces what's out of range instead of making you poll.
          Questions that need you land in one inbox with the context to decide; everything
          proceeding normally proceeds without you. What the system learns, it keeps; what
          happened is queryable. The state of your working life becomes something you inspect
          instead of something you strain to recall — and the place you inspect it is the place
          work starts.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .doors-section {
    padding: 5rem 1.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-text-primary) 4%, transparent);
  }
  .doors-inner {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 3rem;
  }
  .doors-header {
    max-width: 640px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  .doors-headline {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 3.8vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .doors-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .doors-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 860px) {
    .doors-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 3rem;
    }
  }

  .door {
    padding: 2rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-text-primary) 8%, transparent);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background-color: var(--color-bg-elevated);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.125rem;
  }
  .door-title {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .door-body {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
